================================================================================

ARCHITECTURAL REFERENCE

korean-law-mcp — https://github.com/chrisryugj/korean-law-mcp (MIT)

This server is a re-implementation of that project's architecture for a
different jurisdiction and a completely different set of upstreams. What was
taken is design, not data: the two-list tool registry (every tool callable, ten
advertised), the `discover_tools` → `execute_tool` meta-tool pair, the
request-scoped execution budget, the bracket-labelled error taxonomy, the
never-claim-absence rule for a source that could not be reached, the
`legal_research` / `legal_analysis` aggregate entry points, and the two-layer
citation content matcher in `src/lib/citation-content-matcher.ts` (exact
longest-common-substring, then character-bigram Jaccard), which that project in
turn ports from LexDiff.

No Korean legal data, no upstream client and no source adapter is carried over.
Every host in `src/lib/upstream-hosts.ts` is Australian and was researched,
implemented and verified for this project.

